WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.569
Here is an Osprey project. I open

00:02.569 --> 00:04.838
one of its files, then click a

00:04.838 --> 00:07.107
finding in the Deslop sidebar. The file

00:07.107 --> 00:09.377
links lead to the repeated code. I

00:09.377 --> 00:11.646
can review those copies in the same

00:11.646 --> 00:12.942
way as other languages.

00:13.742 --> 00:16.214
I compare the two Osprey files side

00:16.214 --> 00:18.685
by side. I can see the repeated

00:18.685 --> 00:21.156
calculation and check any differences before I

00:21.156 --> 00:23.627
edit it. The useful result is the

00:23.627 --> 00:26.099
source comparison in front of me, with

00:26.099 --> 00:27.864
both copies available to inspect.

00:28.664 --> 00:31.311
This Osprey file uses Python-looking syntax. Its

00:31.311 --> 00:33.958
leading comment tells Deslop how to read

00:33.958 --> 00:36.605
it. I open the file and its

00:36.605 --> 00:39.252
finding to check that it is treated

00:39.252 --> 00:41.899
as Osprey, while ordinary Python files keep

00:41.899 --> 00:43.033
their usual identity.

00:43.833 --> 00:46.269
I open the Osprey project file and

00:46.269 --> 00:48.705
list the Python-looking source files that belong

00:48.705 --> 00:51.141
to it. After saving the setting, I

00:51.141 --> 00:53.577
run Deslop on the project and inspect

00:53.577 --> 00:56.013
the result. That makes the choice visible

00:56.013 --> 00:58.449
in the project instead of leaving it

00:58.449 --> 00:59.145
to guesswork.
